Subject: Compiled field reads by position: qjs_aot_slot_atom_hit

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_slot_atom_hit(recv, slot, atom, &out)`: when the receiver's shape names `atom` at index `slot` as a plain data property (not an accessor, variable reference, auto-init or array length), the value is the same index of the property array, whichever shape the receiver has; the value is borrowed, as at a register read hit. An exotic object, another atom at that index and a lazy value are misses. It is declared in quickjs-aot-rt.h and defined at the end of the engine source.
  - The generator (tools/aot/qjsaot, `qjs_aot_prof_site_atom_slot`) emits it after the site cache's own test for a get_field site whose profiled shapes all put the atom at one slot and whose fills installed own slots: `q2_own_read_hit(...) || qjs_aot_slot_atom_hit(v, slot, atom, &_f)`. QJS_AOT_NO_ATOMSLOT=1 turns it off.

Why we need it
  typescript's AST nodes are built by one constructor chain, so about sixty shapes keep nodeType/type/flags/... at the same index. 87% of the profile's property site executions are at sites that saw four or more shapes (96.6% of those at one slot), which the site cache (one or two shapes) misses and the interpreter served through a frame sync, an op dispatch, the megamorphic cache or a table lookup: 12.4% of cycles below qjs_aot_step.

Soundness
  Exact for any receiver: the shape is read, so a receiver whose shape does not hold the atom at that index misses, and the interpreter's step runs as before. The slot is a hint from the profile and only picks the index to test.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -121014,3 +121014,37 @@
 #undef malloc
 #undef free
 #undef realloc
+
+#if QJS_AOT
+/* An own data property read by position: the receiver's shape names `atom` at index `slot` as a plain data property, so the value is
+   at the same index of the property array, whichever shape the receiver has. A site whose receivers have many shapes that all keep
+   the field at one index (the objects of one hierarchy build their common fields first) is served without a lookup. The value is
+   borrowed, as at a register read hit. Anything else (an exotic object, an accessor, a variable reference, an array length, a lazy
+   value, another atom at that index) is a miss for the caller to take its own way. */
+int qjs_aot_slot_atom_hit(JSValue recv, uint32_t slot, JSAtom atom, JSValue *out)
+{
+    JSObject *p;
+    JSShape *sh;
+    JSShapeProperty *prs;
+    JSValue v;
+
+    if (unlikely(JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(recv) != JS_TAG_OBJECT))
+        return 0;
+    p = JS_VALUE_GET_OBJ(recv);
+    sh = p->shape;
+    if (unlikely(p->is_exotic || slot >= (uint32_t)sh->prop_count))
+        return 0;
+    prs = &get_shape_prop(sh)[slot];
+    if (unlikely(prs->atom != atom || (prs->flags & (JS_PROP_TMASK | JS_PROP_LENGTH))))
+        return 0;
+    v = p->prop[slot].u.value;
+    if (unlikely(JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) == JS_TAG_LAZY_JSON))
+        return 0;
+#if !QJS_DRC
+    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) < 0)
+        return 0;
+#endif
+    *out = v;
+    return 1;
+}
+#endif
